se they overlap. So I need to choose one chow and use the remaining W tile with others.  \n\nSuppose I keep chow W2,W3,W4. Then leftover: W5, B5,B5, J1,J1. I need one more meld and a pair. Possible:  \n- Pair: B5 pair or J1 pair.  \n- Melds: Could be pung of J1 (need one more J1) or pung of B5 (need one more B5) or chow involving W5: W5,W6,W7 (need W6,W7). W7 unshown 0 (all gone? Unshown W7:0, W6:1, W8:1, etc). So W5 chow is unlikely. So likely aim for pung.  \n\nIf I discard a W tile, I break the chow possibilities. If I discard a J1 or B5, I reduce chance for pung.  \n\nBetter to discard a W tile that is less useful. Which W tile?  \nW2, W3, W4, W5 all are part of chows. But if I discard one, I might still form chow with the others. For example, if I discard W2, I can have chow W3,W4,W5. If I discard W5, chow W2,W3,W4. If I discard W3 or W4, I break both possible chows. So discard W2 or W5 is safer.  \n\nAlso consider defensiveness: Look at opponents' discards and melds.  \nNext player has pung of F1, and their discards include B5, W6, J3, etc. They might be close to winning.  \nPlayer across has chow W4,W5,W6, discards include many terminals.  \nPrevious player has no melds.  \n\nMy own history: I discarded many tiles, including B4, B1, W1, etc.  \n\nGiven I have two pairs (B5 and J1), I might aim for a hand like: two chows (already have two), one pung (of B5 or J1), and one pair (the other). So I need to complete a pung. Which pung is easier? J1 has 4 left, B5 has 3 left. J1 might be safer because dragons are often discarded late? But no J1 discarded yet, so they might be held. B5 has been discarded once (by next player), so maybe more likely to be discarded again.  \n\nBut also, I have two chows already exposed (W7,W8,W9 and T6,T7,T8). So my hand is mixed. Potential scoring patterns:  \n- All Chows (if I form two more chows) but I have pung possibility.  \n- All Simples? My hand has no terminals or honors except J1. J1 is honor, so not All Simples.  \n- Half Fl